sentences.t:
Αἶαν, παῖ Τελαμῶνος ἀμύμονος, οὐκ ἄρ’ ἔμελλες
οὐδὲ θανὼν λήσεσθαι ἐμοὶ χόλου εἵνεκα τευχέων
οὐλομένων;

sentences.tr:
Aian, pai Telamōnos amymonos, ouk ar’ emelles
oude thanōn lēsesthai emoi cholou heineka teucheōn
oulomenōn;

sentences.l:
and for you we Achaeans,
equally with the head of Achilles, Peleus’ son,
grieve, now that you have perished, continually;

and he lay over nine plethra,
and two vultures, one on either side seated beside him, were tearing his liver,
going into the caul within, and he did not ward them off with his hands;

And high-leaved trees poured fruit down from over his head:
pear trees and pomegranates and apple trees with splendid fruit,
and sweet fig trees and thriving olives;
